  <!-- The decoder behind rav1g.h is the Rust library of the Cargo workspace (crates/rav1g with its
       interface for C; docs/rust-port.md, step 5), built before this project and given to it by one of
       two properties. A build without either stops with a message (the target at the end of this file).

       /p:Rav1gRustDll=<directory>: the library as a DLL (rav1g.dll with rav1g.dll.lib), which is in
       the package next to the program: the build whose C is compiled by clang with link time
       optimisation, which only lld-link can link. This is the default package of CI.

       /p:Rav1gRustLib=<directory>: the static library, rav1g.lib, with the library of its C
       (rav1g_c.lib: the shader table; objects of /GL, compiled by this link). The functions of
       Windows have to come from the API sets of WindowsApp.lib, as they do for the rest of the
       player: a program that imports from kernel32.dll may not load in the console's app container.
       Two things see to that. rav1g.lib is given without the import members for desktop DLLs that
       the windows crates put into it (testing/uwp_imports/prune_import_members.py): the linker
       looks for a function first in the library that asked for it. And WindowsApp.lib stands right
       behind the decoder's libraries, in front of ntdll.lib, userenv.lib and kernel32.lib, which
       are there for functions of Rust's standard library that are in the objects the linker loads
       and not in the program it writes (file and pipe calls, the home directory, QueryDosDeviceW).
       The check of the program's imports in CI (testing/uwp_imports) fails when a function of
       kernel32.dll does get into the program. -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Rav1gRustLib)'!=''">
    <Link>
      <AdditionalDependencies>$(Rav1gRustLib)\rav1g.lib;$(Rav1gRustLib)\rav1g_c.lib;WindowsApp.lib;ntdll.lib;userenv.lib;kernel32.lib;%(AdditionalDependencies)</AdditionalDependencies>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Rav1gRustDll)'!=''">
    <Link>
      <AdditionalDependencies>$(Rav1gRustDll)\rav1g.dll.lib;%(AdditionalDependencies)</AdditionalDependencies>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(Rav1gRustDll)'!=''">
    <None Include="$(Rav1gRustDll)\rav1g.dll">
      <Link>rav1g.dll</Link>
      <DeploymentContent>true</DeploymentContent>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>
